    "slot_number": "slot-6-1-1",
    "slot_key": "Authorization rules for issuing commands and changing settings",
    "slot_value": "Only authorized parties may use the communication system to carry commands or settings back to individual turbines or wind-farm controllers, and remote commands or settings changes should only be allowed when the turbine or farm controller is in a normal supervised operating state and the operator is authenticated and authorized. Operators should be able to monitor the fleet, acknowledge alarms, and issue the normal operational commands they are trained for; maintenance staff should have broader access to diagnostics and some configuration or test functions, but only for the assets they are responsible for and usually during an agreed maintenance window; external service providers should have the most limited access, mostly read-only or tightly scoped service actions; central operations should retain control over higher-risk commands, access permissions, and any changes that affect multiple turbines or site-wide behavior. People can act only within their assigned assets and role scope, and anything that crosses into site-wide or multi-turbine impact needs central operations approval; commands that could affect other turbines, the farm controller, or a safety-critical state are restricted even if the person has access to the individual turbine. The system should check both asset ownership and command impact before allowing it, and block anything that exceeds the user's authorization. New operators should be role-restricted right away, with only low-risk actions allowed until they have been trained and approved. Full command capability would usually be reserved for sites where the communication path, responsibilities, and safety handling are all agreed and tested end to end. Supervision-only is appropriate when the local operator or owner has not approved remote control, when command-path confidence and safety handling are insufficient, when the site is still being commissioned, when vendor integration is incomplete, or when regulatory or contractual restrictions limit who may issue commands. Full command capability requires an explicit operating agreement, validated command safety checks, and confirmation that local fallback behavior remains independent and safe if communication fails.",
